  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA general unsettled pattern to bring isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms, possibly severe, through tonight. High amounts of\ninstability, mostly elevated, will continue through tonight.\nMeanwhile a weak pseudo warm front near a developing low will \nbe draped across much of the state through tonight. Aloft sees \nfalling heights as the upper level ridge moves east and \nsouthwest flow takes its place. Models also indicate a low level\njet may return this evening through tonight. All this combined \nwill return widespread chances for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough tonight. Today's storms have left plenty of remnant \nboundaries across the state, How these interact with each other \ncould influence severe weather tonight as well. Overall the \nstronger severe weather will be located to anywhere with higher \nshear which currently analyzed in the north and west. Elsewhere \nhas less shear, although high amounts of instability may be just\nenough for stronger pulse like storms. Overall a hail threat up\nto golf ball size will be possible with any stronger storm \nthrough tonight, with rotating storms in the higher sheared \nareas having the better potential for the bigger hail. The wind \nthreat is modest through tonight. DCAPE is high, although 0 to 3\nkm shear is lacking as are low level lapse rates. Thus winds 60\nto perhaps 70 mph are possible. There still remains a low \ntornado threat through tonight. This is highly dependent on \nboundary interactions, although there is some increased SRH and \n0 to 1 KM shear through tonight. Of note are some increased non \nsupercell parameters through this evening. Overall confidence in\nsurface based storms through tonight is low, yet a tornado is \npossible along any boundary interactions. High pwats over 1.5 \ninches will continue to bring a heavy rain and localized \nflooding threat. If confidence in any one area for heavy rain \nincrease then perhaps a flood watch may be needed tonight and \nperhaps again on Monday. Otherwise look for mild lows in the 60s\ntonight. Patchy fog may also return tonight to areas without \nthunderstorms and some clearing. Confidence was not high enough \nto include fog at this time.\n\nSurface low then moves across the state on Labor Day bringing\nmore isolated to scattered severe thunderstorms. Timing wise,\nsevere storms could be ongoing in the morning as a low level jet\ncontinues ahead of the low in the morning. As the low pushes\nthrough following by a cold front, thunderstorms could continue\nthrough the day from west to east through the day. A few\nthunderstorms could then develop along a trailing front tomorrow\nevening, although this may be more uncertain depending how much\nthe daytime convection impacts the atmosphere. All hazards\nremain possible. Increased shear and instability will support\nlarge hail to golf ball sized. Meanwhile, the potential for line\ndevelopment could bring strong winds to 70 mph. There also\nremains a conditional tornado threat for Monday as dewpoints\nremain high and a weak warm front lifts ahead of the low. \nStorms would have to be surface based to increase the tornado \nthreat. Currently SPC has a Marginal Risk for severe weather. \nCSU-MLP severe outlooks shown all threats possible for Monday. \nThus anyone with outdoor plans for Monday should continue to be \nweather aware on Monday. Highs for Labor Day will continue to be\nwarm with 80s for most of the area, and 70s north. Breezy \nsoutherly winds will be found until a breezy westerly wind moves\nin behind the low. Precipitation diminishes Monday night, with \nlows cooling in the 50s to near 60. \n\nThe main concern for Tuesday remains strong winds. Both the NBM\nand ECMWF EFI support advisory criteria winds of sustained \nspeeds between 30 and 40 mph and gusts of 45 to 55 mph. Tuesday \nwill be much cooler with highs from the upper 60s northwest to \nlower 80s southeast, but also drier with minimum humidity \nfalling to around 25 to 35 percent. The combination of strong \nwinds and lower RH will likely bring near- critical to critical fire\nweather conditions to all of southwest and some south central \nNorth Dakota, with critical conditions especially possible in \nthe far southwest where RH could fall closer to 20 to 25 \npercent. A few showers may also be found Tuesday, mainly in the\nnorth. Cooler temperatures remain in the forecast on Wednesday,\nbut with winds not nearly as strong yet still breezy at times.  \nEnsembles have now trended toward a quick warmup on Thursday due\nto a transient ridge, followed by a distinct cooling trend \nheading into next weekend, but with increasing spread due to \nsynoptic scale wave phasing uncertainty. There could be an\nincreased fire weather threat on Thursday, especially in the\nsouthwest. Limited chances for precipitation also look to be\nWednesday through Friday.",
